2. Line 9-21: multiple `revised_at` keys (YAML duplicate keys!) — lines 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 18, 21 all define `revised_at`. In YAML, duplicate mapping keys: last one wins in PyYAML (with a warning), and many parsers error. The revision history is thus encoded as repeated keys with comments. `revision: 9` while the comment at line 19 says "rev9 delta". Hmm, but revision 9 corresponds to revised_at 00:15:00 (line 21). This is sloppy but the effective value is the last. Note `revised_at_unused` at line 23. This is a real structural defect: duplicate keys make parsers disagree (some reject, some take first, some last). Under the rubric's machine_checkable schema checks ("artifact bytes + sha256 + one linter verdict"), duplicate keys could be a hard failure or a minor finding. It also means the "revised_at" is ambiguous. Worth flagging — though maybe prior reviewers already did? Let me check F2b-review-18 and the softflag disposition. Actually, also `class_contract_pointer` points to `artifacts/formulation/formulation_taxonomy.yaml#class_contracts.AF-SCC-C0-VAC-GEN` — the authoring tree, not the canonical `research_map/formulation_taxonomy.yaml`. The canonical-path policy (ASTRA_HANDOFF) says "the canonical path is authoritative (`schemas/*.yaml`, `research_map/formulation_taxonomy.yaml`); `artifacts/formulation/**` is the authoring tree". So the pointer to the authoring tree diverges from policy — a real finding (dangling class-core reference?). The falsifier in assignment astra-indep-1-F1-F2-formulation says "A revision that still contains a dangling class-core reference, a composite 'C0 or C2' string, or a per-class quantifier that disagrees with its own statement_formal is rejected on re-review." The `class_contract_pointer` points to the authoring taxonomy which still exists (perhaps equal), but the declared F0 binding hash is stale.

